# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the WEK test suites.
#
# Referenced by tools/scripts/preflight.sh's two ASAN/UBSAN legs (the opt-in
# --sanitize= leg and the default gate's asan-gate step) via:
#   LSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=$_PF_DIR/lsan.supp" ...
#
# Scope: one verified entry per known system-library leak, each backed by a
# captured stack trace -- never a broad first-party prefix. A `leak:<name>`
# entry matches by function name ANYWHERE in the allocation stack, across any
# caller, not just the call site that first surfaced it -- keep that in mind
# before assuming an entry here is scoped to a single call site.
#
# Format: https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/AddressSanitizerLeakSanitizer#suppressions

# libmpv's render-context bring-up strdup()s a short device/driver name string
# inside libdrm and never frees it -- confirmed by stack trace to be a
# system-library leak, not first-party code (every allocating frame sits
# inside libdrm.so.2 / libmpv.so.2). Reachable from three call sites in this
# tree, all through libmpv's public mpv_render_context_create() API:
# wallpaper::HWVideoTextureDecoder::open() (HWVideoTextureDecoder.cpp),
# VideoTextureDecoder::open()'s software fallback (VideoTextureDecoder.cpp),
# and CreateMpvContex() (MpvBackend.cpp). Confirmed via backend_scene_tests'
# "a destroyed decoder holds no dma-buf from the frames it rendered" case.
leak:mpv_render_context_create

# Fontconfig 2.18.3 retains 895 B / 9 allocations from parsing its implicit
# global configuration. A library-only FcConfigSubstitute(NULL, ...) probe
# reproduces this after destroying the request pattern; 100 calls retain the
# same amount. FcFini clears it, but a Plasma plugin does not own global state.
# Match config loading, not all FcPattern allocations or first-party callers.
# This also excludes config-parser allocations if a caller leaks an explicitly
# owned FcConfig; ordinary request/match-pattern leaks remain detectable.
# See issue #41. No DBus/NVIDIA suppression: unloaded frames cannot be matched
# reliably without also hiding allocations unrelated to the verified driver.
leak:FcConfigParseAndLoad
